---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["how-to-calculate-overtime-pay"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    Overtime pay is your regular rate multiplied by an overtime rate — usually
    time and a half — for the hours over a threshold. The arithmetic is simple;
    the parts that need care are the <em>regular rate</em> and the
    <em>threshold</em>, both of which depend on your situation and location.
  </p>

  <h2>The formula</h2>
  <p>
    <code>overtime rate = regular hourly rate &times; multiplier</code><br />
    <code>overtime pay = overtime rate &times; overtime hours</code><br />
    <code>gross pay = regular hours &times; regular rate + overtime pay</code>
  </p>
  <p>
    Example: $20 an hour, 46 hours in the week, US federal rule. 40 hours at $20
    is $800. 6 overtime hours at $30 (1.5 &times; $20) is $180. Gross pay is
    <strong>$980</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>What "time and a half" means</h2>
  <p>
    1.5 times the regular rate. On $18 an hour that is $27. "Double time" is 2&times;
    ($36) — where it applies at all; see
    <a href="/guides/what-is-double-time-pay/">what is double-time pay</a>. Some
    awards and contracts use other multipliers.
  </p>

  <h2>The regular rate is not always your hourly wage</h2>
  <p>
    Under the FLSA, the "regular rate" includes almost all compensation for the
    week, not just your base wage. It must be recalculated when your pay is not a
    simple hourly amount:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Salaried non-exempt:</strong> divide the weekly salary by the
      hours it is intended to cover. A $800/week salary for a 40-hour week is a
      $20 regular rate; overtime hours are then paid at $30 on top.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Two pay rates in one week:</strong> the regular rate is the
      weighted average. 20 hours at $18 and 25 hours at $22 = <code>(360 + 550)
      &divide; 45 = $20.22</code>; the 5 overtime hours get an extra 0.5 &times;
      $20.22 on top of their straight-time pay.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Non-discretionary bonuses and commission:</strong> a production or
      attendance bonus is added into the regular rate for the weeks it covers,
      which raises the overtime rate for those weeks. A $100 weekly bonus on 50
      hours adds $2/hour to the regular rate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shift differentials</strong> (e.g. +$1.50/hour for nights) are
      part of the regular rate too.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Excluded:</strong> discretionary bonuses, gifts, paid-leave
      cash-outs, and reimbursements do not enter the regular rate.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Worked example: salaried non-exempt with a bonus</h2>
  <p>
    Salary $1,000/week for 40 hours, plus a $150 non-discretionary bonus, 48
    hours worked:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Straight-time compensation: $1,000 + $150 = $1,150 for 48 hours.</li>
    <li>Regular rate: <code>1150 &divide; 48 = $23.96</code>.</li>
    <li>Overtime premium: <code>0.5 &times; 23.96 &times; 8 = $95.84</code>.</li>
    <li>Total: <code>1150 + 95.84 = $1,245.84</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Weekly vs daily thresholds</h2>
  <p>
    US federal law counts overtime by the <strong>workweek</strong> — over 40
    hours in a fixed seven-day span — not by the day or the pay period. Working
    12 hours on Monday and 4 hours a day the rest of the week (28 total) is 40
    hours: no federal overtime. Some states add daily overtime:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Overtime threshold</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>US — federal (FLSA)</td><td>Over 40 hours / week</td><td>1.5&times;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>US — California</td><td>Over 8 / day or 40 / week; over 12 / day</td><td>1.5&times; then 2&times;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>US — Alaska, Nevada</td><td>Over 8 hours / day (conditions apply)</td><td>1.5&times;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Canada — Ontario</td><td>Over 44 hours / week</td><td>1.5&times;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Canada — federal</td><td>Over 40 hours / week</td><td>1.5&times;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>No statutory overtime rate — set by contract</td><td>Contractual</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Australia</td><td>Set by the relevant modern award (often 38-hour week)</td><td>Award-based, often 1.5&times; then 2&times;</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These are calculation starting points, <strong>not legal advice</strong> —
    your entitlement depends on your classification, award or contract, and
    current law. The UK's 48-hour Working Time limit caps <em>average</em>
    weekly hours for health and safety; it does not require overtime pay.
  </p>

  <h2>Do salaried employees get overtime?</h2>
  <p>
    Often not. Under the FLSA, employees classified as <em>exempt</em> — which
    generally requires being paid on a salary basis, above the salary threshold,
    and primarily performing exempt duties — are not entitled to overtime.
    <em>Non-exempt</em> employees are, whether paid hourly or by salary. The
    full test is in
    <a href="/guides/exempt-vs-non-exempt-employees/">exempt vs non-exempt
    employees</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Comp time, and being paid "straight time" for overtime</h2>
  <p>
    Private-sector employers generally cannot swap overtime pay for time off
    ("comp time") — that is only lawful for many public-sector employers. Paying
    non-exempt overtime at straight time, or averaging hours across two weeks to
    avoid a 40-hour week, is also not allowed.
  </p>

  <h2>Is overtime taxed more?</h2>
  <p>
    No — overtime is taxed like any wages. A large paycheck can be
    over-withheld because payroll annualises it, but that evens out at tax time.
    A temporary 2025&ndash;2028 federal deduction for the overtime premium also
    exists. See <a href="/guides/is-overtime-taxed-more/">is overtime taxed
    more?</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Does overtime start after 40 or 44 hours?</h3>
  <p>
    40 under US federal law and Canadian federal law; 44 in Ontario. Your
    province, state, or contract may differ.
  </p>
  <h3>Is a holiday or vacation day "hours worked" for overtime?</h3>
  <p>
    Under federal law, no — only hours actually worked count toward the 40.
    Some contracts are more generous.
  </p>

  <h2>Calculators</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/calculators/overtime-calculator/">Overtime Calculator</a> — enter your rate and hours, pick a rule or set your own multiplier and threshold.</li>
    <li><a href="/calculators/time-card-calculator/">Time Card Calculator</a> — a full week of shifts with the daily and weekly split applied.</li>
    <li><a href="/calculators/hourly-pay-calculator/">Hourly Pay Calculator</a> — gross pay projections including overtime.</li>
  </ul>

